## Reverse UTF-8 String in Go

Here's a clean implementation that correctly handles multi-byte UTF-8 characters (runes):

```go
package main

import "fmt"

// Reverse returns the UTF-8 string s reversed.
func Reverse(s string) string {
	runes := []rune(s)

	left, right := 0, len(runes)-1
	for left < right {
		runes[left], runes[right] = runes[right], runes[left]
		left++
		right--
	}

	return string(runes)
}

func main() {
	tests := []string{
		"Hello, World!",
		"Hello, 世界",
		"🙂🙃😀",
		"",
		"a",
		"racecar",
	}

	for _, s := range tests {
		fmt.Printf("%-20q => %q\n", s, Reverse(s))
	}
}
```

### Output

```
"Hello, World!"      => "!dlroW ,olleH"
"Hello, 世界"         => "界世 ,olleH"
"🙂🙃😀"              => "😀🙃🙂"
""                   => ""
"a"                  => "a"
"racecar"            => "racecar"
```

---

## Why `[]rune` Instead of `[]byte`?

| Approach | Issue |
|---|---|
| `[]byte` | Reverses raw bytes, corrupting multi-byte characters |
| `[]rune` | Reverses Unicode code points correctly ✅ |

### Example of the bug with `[]byte`:
```go
// ❌ WRONG - corrupts multi-byte characters
func ReverseBad(s string) string {
    b := []byte(s)
    for i, j := 0, len(b)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        b[i], b[j] = b[j], b[i]
    }
    return string(b)
}

// ReverseBad("世界") => garbled output (invalid UTF-8)
```

---

## Alternative: Using `range` (More Idiomatic)

```go
func Reverse(s string) string {
	runes := make([]rune, 0, len(s))
	for _, r := range s { // range decodes UTF-8 runes automatically
		runes = append(runes, r)
	}

	for i, j := 0, len(runes)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
		runes[i], runes[j] = runes[j], runes[i]
	}

	return string(runes)
}
```

Both approaches are correct. The first (`[]rune(s)`) is more concise, while the second makes the UTF-8 decoding step more explicit.

