### The `WorkerPool` Function

This function takes a receive-only channel of jobs and the number of workers `n`. It uses a `sync.WaitGroup` to ensure that the function doesn't return until all jobs are processed and all workers have finished.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
	"time"
)

// WorkerPool spawns n goroutines (workers) to process jobs from the jobs channel.
// It waits for all workers to finish before returning.
func WorkerPool(jobs <-chan int, n int) {
	// A WaitGroup is used to wait for all spawned goroutines to complete.
	var wg sync.WaitGroup

	// Spawn n worker goroutines.
	for i := 1; i <= n; i++ {
		// Increment the WaitGroup counter for each new goroutine.
		wg.Add(1)

		// Launch a new goroutine.
		// We pass the worker ID 'i' as an argument to the goroutine's function
		// to ensure each goroutine gets its own unique ID.
		go func(workerID int) {
			// Decrement the WaitGroup counter when the goroutine exits.
			// 'defer' ensures this happens regardless of how the function returns.
			defer wg.Done()

			// The core of the worker: a loop that reads from the 'jobs' channel.
			// The 'for range' loop on a channel will automatically break
			// when the channel is closed and all values have been received.
			for job := range jobs {
				fmt.Printf("Worker %d started  job %d\n", workerID, job)
				// Simulate work by sleeping for a second.
				time.Sleep(time.Second)
				fmt.Printf("Worker %d finished job %d\n", workerID, job)
			}
		}(i)
	}

	// Block until the WaitGroup counter goes back to 0,
	// which means all workers have called wg.Done().
	wg.Wait()
}
```

